In article <29763@shemp.CS.UCLA.EDU> cccph@eel.cs.ucla.edu (Charles Hobbs) writes:
>
>I downloaded the PC Nethack binary from lampwick.berkeley.edu, and 
>have been playing it on an IBM PS/2 Model 50Z with 1 MB of memory.
>In general, I'm pleased with it, but it tends to access the disk too much
>(I improved the performance somewhat by using a ram cache on the hard disk)

Yeah, there's something strange going on; performance seems to be
significantly lower than in alphatest versions. A RAM cache will help,
a ramdisk far less so (for some reason).

>I also noticed that it only uses less than 300 K of memory, on average, at
>any one time.,

This cannot be true. But then, except for a few arcane developers'
TSRs, there is no way of measuring this anyway. Perhaps what you mean
to say is that if you use the shell command, '!', then the overlay
system manages to _free up_ all but 300K. The memory that is made
available to the operating system when your game is suspended and the
memory that is used as overlay area by the game are completely
independent. If the game is playable at all, _well_ over 450K must be
in use while the game is active.

>Perhaps, actions that are frequently used (moving, fighting, eating, etc.)
>could be memory resident at all times, while less frequently used
>commands ("#" extended commands) can be treated as overlays.

Actually we had to put almost _everything_ in overlays to make the
thing go at all. But the replacement strategy is Least Recently Used,
which should be very close to optimal (there are still changes we want
to make, and performance should still be improvable, but we are _well_
beyond the point you suggest). To put this another way: because the
overlay manager we wrote is a bit more intelligent in its use of
memory than most, the _more_ stuff we put in overlays, the _less_ disk
access there is because the amount of dynamic adapatability of the
programme as a whole is increased.

Any assistance that anyone has to offer on the overlay scheme (either
in the arrangement of the overlays or in the design of the overlay
manager module) is entirely welcome, but please note that we have
already developed custom tools for supporting this programme whose
performance far exceeds that of those that are commercially available.
In particular, you WILL want to read the documentation in the file
ovlmgr.doc before starting to hack, or the results of your work will
be both disappointing and very hard to interpret.

The techniques employed in ovmgr.asm range from the third to fifth
arcana. Check you Hacking stat and be warned!
